===============================================================================
DEFENSIVE-RESEARCH RIDER — ATTACK-SIMULATION MODULES
===============================================================================

This rider applies to the following modules and to any derivative work that
reuses, copies, or modifies them:

    src/attacks/dns_tunneling.py
    src/attacks/timing_side_channel.py
    src/attacks/quic_congestion.py
    src/attacks/cert_injection.py
    src/attacks/padding_oracle.py
    and the associated data file:
    data/attack_matrix.csv

These modules exist solely to support the reproducibility and independent
verification of the defensive results reported in Chapter 5 of the thesis
"Next-Generation Secure Encrypted DNS Architectures: Four Complementary
Frameworks for Adaptive, Privacy-Preserving, and Quantum-Resilient Defense."
They simulate attack techniques inside an isolated network namespace so that
a reviewer can verify that the detection and mitigation mechanisms described
in the thesis fire as claimed.

By using, copying, modifying, or distributing these modules, you agree to
the following conditions:

  1. Permitted use. These modules may be used only for defensive security
     research, academic reproducibility, and controlled testing on systems
     and networks that you own or have explicit written authorization to
     test. "Authorization" means authorization from the party that owns or
     operates the system, and, where required, from the relevant institutional
     review board or equivalent authority.

  2. Prohibited use. These modules must not be used, in whole or in part,
     against any system or network that you do not own or have explicit
     written authorization to test. They must not be used to develop,
     distribute, or operate offensive tooling. They must not be used in any
     manner that violates applicable law in your jurisdiction.

  3. Isolation requirement. The modules are designed to run inside an
     isolated network namespace (see configs/attack_suite_params.yaml,
     key `isolation`). The isolation must not be disabled when the modules
     are executed. Disabling isolation shifts the responsibility for any
     resulting traffic entirely onto the party that disabled it.

  4. No warranty. The modules are provided "as is", under the same warranty
     disclaimer as the base license above. The authors accept no liability
     for any direct, indirect, incidental, special, exemplary, or
     consequential damages arising from the use or misuse of these modules.

  5. Attribution. Any academic publication that uses these modules must cite
     the thesis. The citation is given in the repository's README.md §14.

  6. Severability. If any provision of this rider is held unenforceable, the
     remaining provisions remain in full force and effect. The base MIT
     license above continues to apply to every module of the artifact that
     is not listed at the top of this rider.

Nothing in this rider is intended to restrict the rights granted to you under
the base MIT license for the rest of the artifact. The rider is an additional
condition on the attack-simulation modules only.

===============================================================================
DATASET LICENSING NOTICE
===============================================================================

The artifact does not redistribute any dataset. The three external datasets
used by Chapter 5 (DS-1, DS-2, DS-3; see STATUS.md §4 and README.md §4) are
each governed by their own license and terms of use. Users of this artifact
must comply with those licenses when downloading and using the datasets.
Fetching the datasets is done by data/download_datasets.py, which verifies a
SHA-256 hash for each archive against a committed manifest before the data is
used.
